      "dom_id": "question-112321",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $7|2x-5|+1=15,$ then $x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7|2x-5|+1 & =15 \\\\ 7|2x-5| & =14 \\\\ |2x-5| & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|2x-5|=2x-5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =2 \\\\ 2x-5 & =2 \\\\ 2x & =7 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2x-5|=-(2x-5),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =2 \\\\ -(2x-5) & =2 \\\\ -2x+5 & =2 \\\\ -2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{7}{2}.$",
